e  institution itself and make themselves  heard  [Music]  in response to protesters police have  either moved people on or in some  instances made arrests two people in  edinburgh have been charged  so i called out who elected him  after saying that at a proclamation of  the king in oxford on sunday simon hill  was handcuffed and detained by the  police are we really now in a place in  21st century britain where a new head of  state is proclaimed uh we're told to  accept this person without without any  question and somebody who speaks out  against it is  he is dragged off and arrested it felt  like a  like i'd slipped into the 16th century  for a minute  policing events leading up to the  queen's funeral is a large and complex  operation and there are new laws around  protests  the police crime sentencing and courts  act which became law this year gives  police more powers to disrupt protests  if they're deemed to be causing  significant harm to those nearby  interpretation of that depends on the  circumstances and the sensitivities and  the complication here is we have two  things happening at once a moment of  national mourning but also the  proclamation of a new king a big  constitutional change  well this is absolutely the time to talk  about the future of the monarchy because  we have a pneumonic and  you know charles has become king without  consent without discussion or debate  it's just happened automatically  and there is  no effort to have a serious debate about  that and that is completely wrong  but people coming to pay their respects  in windsor say this isn't the week for  it  they wouldn't do it would they if the  lady down the road died would they  yeah so you're talking about the monarch  of this country has been around for 70  years  i think that's just disrespectful some  people might be saying well they're  protesting against the new king not  against them well i think i think at the  end of the day  whoever it is  you know whether it's politic